leaves the front matter.

## Introduction

Points belong to every cell by a softmax over the squared distance to
each centre, with a per-tessellation bandwidth tau under an Exponential
prior, in place of the hard nearest-centre rule; as tau falls to 0 the
hard assignment returns. This is the Voronoi analogue of soft BART
(Linero and Yang 2018), the kernel acting on the distance to each centre
rather than on a chain of split gates. Reach for it when the truth is
smooth and the ensemble is small, the setting soft BART was built for.

Prerequisite: BART (Chipman, George and McCulloch 2010) and AddiVortes
(Stone and Gosling 2025).

## The model

The four display lines of `docs/models.md`, "Soft membership": the
weights, the cell function, the prior on tau, and the rate default of
10 (the SoftBart `tau_rate` default, a prior mean bandwidth of a tenth
of a column's range). Link to `crates/thiessen/src/cells.rs`.

## Example

```{r example}
source("_setup.R")
data <- smooth_surface(n = 300, sd = 0.1, seed = 1)
control <- thiessen_control(tessellations = 20, general_params = schedule)
soft <- thiessen_control(
  tessellations = 20, general_params = schedule,
  mean_params = term_params(geometry = geometry_params(membership = soft_membership()))
)
fits <- paired_fits(data, list(default = control, soft = soft), seeds)
summary(fits$soft[[1]])
score(fits, data)
```

m = 20 so the piecewise-constant steps are visible. No external
comparator: nothing on CRAN softens a tessellation, and SoftBart on
trees would compare the geometry rather than the softening.

## What it shows

Figure 1, mechanism: a transect at x2 = 0.5, truth, hard fit, soft fit,
the steps of the hard fit visible.

```{r mechanism}
```

Figure 2, comparison: paired RMSE and interval width per seed, default
against soft, beside the table above; second panel, the posterior of
`bandwidth[j]` from `posterior::as_draws_df()` against its
Exponential(10) prior.

```{r comparison}
```

Figure 3, boundary: the RMSE gap against m in {20, 50, 200}; the
advantage closes as the hard ensemble gets the cells it needs.

```{r boundary}
```

## Validity

Stem `soft`: the SBC rank-ECDF difference and Geweke plots from the
nightly calibration battery, copied by `tools/calibration-figures.R`,
with the run in the caption. Validation is the battery; this page is
illustration. Mention the known-answer tests of the marginal and the
joint draw and the broken-sampler fixture that drops the determinant
term.

```{r battery, results = "asis"}
cat(readLines("figures/soft-membership/battery.md"), sep = "\n")
```

![SBC rank-ECDF difference](figures/soft-membership/sbc_ecdf_diff_soft.png)

![Geweke](figures/soft-membership/geweke_ecdf_soft.png)

## Limits

State only what the module note supports. Candidates: the kernel is on
the distance to each centre, and the smoothness and adaptation results
of Linero and Yang (2018, Theorems 1 to 3) are for gated splits; the
Exponential prior and its rate are borrowed from SoftBart with no
Voronoi-specific argument, and the interaction between tau and sigma_c
is unstudied; the empty-cell rule still counts nearest-centre members;
no weighted update exists for the linear basis and the variance
ensemble's inverse-gamma cells have no closed-form weighted conditional,
so both compositions are rejected at validation.

## References

Chipman, H. A., George, E. I. and McCulloch, R. E. (2010). BART:
Bayesian additive regression trees. *The Annals of Applied Statistics*
4(1), 266-298. doi:10.1214/09-AOAS285

Linero, A. R. and Yang, Y. (2018). Bayesian regression tree ensembles
that adapt to smoothness and sparsity. *Journal of the Royal
Statistical Society Series B* 80(5), 1087-1110. doi:10.1111/rssb.12293

Stone, A. and Gosling, J. P. (2025). AddiVortes: (Bayesian) additive
Voronoi tessellations. *Journal of Computational and Graphical
Statistics* 34(3), 859-871. doi:10.1080/10618600.2024.2414104
